    <briefdescription>
<para>C++20 concept satisfied by the wrappers of a <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput>, whose condition is ambiguous. </para>
    </briefdescription>
    <detaileddescription>
<para>The condition of a <computeroutput>std::optional&lt;bool&gt;</computeroutput>, or of a smart pointer to a <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput>, tests the wrapper (a value is present, or the pointer is not null), not the <computeroutput>bool</computeroutput> that it holds, which is what a reader of <computeroutput>t.expect (flag)</computeroutput> assumes; a check with <computeroutput>false</computeroutput> inside would pass, and be reported as <computeroutput>true</computeroutput>, for another reason than the one the author had in mind, as the checks of the arguments that are always true (see <computeroutput>suspicious_condition</computeroutput>). A check that passes silently by mistake is worse than one that does not compile, so <computeroutput>extension::checkable</computeroutput> rejects these types, and the application writes <computeroutput>has_value()</computeroutput>, <computeroutput>!= nullptr</computeroutput>, or compares the value with <computeroutput>eq()</computeroutput>. The wrappers of the other types are accepted, since the presence of a value is then the only meaning of the condition.</para>
